<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The Android launch window, painted before React Native has drawn anything.

  Two layers:

   1. `sanket_launch_background` — pure black, matching the intro clip's own
      background. This is the letterbox, and it is also what shows on the part
      of the window the poster does not cover.
   2. The supplied splash poster, centred and **unscaled**.

  Why `gravity="center"` and not `fill` or a stretched `scaleType`:

  The poster is 1080x1920 (9:16) but phones are taller — this device is
  1080x2400 (20:9). `fill` would stretch the artwork vertically by a quarter and
  visibly deform the ring and the संकेत वाणी wordmark. `center` draws it at its
  native size, centred, letterboxed against the black layer — which is exactly
  what `IntroScreen` paints with `<Image resizeMode="contain">` over the same
  black. On this device the two are pixel-identical, so the hand-off from the
  launch window to the first React Native frame is invisible.

  Trade-off, stated honestly: on a display narrower than 1080 px `center` crops
  rather than scales, so a small device would see the middle of the poster. It
  is still branded artwork over black rather than an empty panel, which is the
  failure this replaces. Making it scale would require a per-density copy of the
  PNG, i.e. re-encoding the supplied asset, which is out of scope.

  The bitmap lives in `drawable-nodpi` deliberately. In a density bucket Android
  would treat a 1080x1920 PNG as mdpi artwork and scale it up ~3x on this
  handset, which is the classic launch-poster blur.

  See res/values/styles.xml for where this is applied, and res/values/colors.xml
  for why the black is #000000 rather than Home's #050506.
-->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item android:drawable="@color/sanket_launch_background" />
    <item>
        <bitmap
            android:src="@drawable/sanket_launch_poster"
            android:gravity="center" />
    </item>
</layer-list>
